## Palm Springs Lemon Law FAQs

## What qualifies for Palm Springs lemon law?

Any defective vehicle sold with a manufacturer or dealer warranty may qualify under California lemon law - as long as the defect was reported within the terms of the warranty. These terms can vary by brand, but many cover vehicles for up to 3 years or 36,000 miles. Be sure to check your warranty terms.

## Can I file a Palm Springs lemon law claim without an attorney?

Technically, anyone can file a lemon claim on their own. However, manufacturers often deny consumer-filed claims - even if they are legitimate. Automakers use many different tactics to avoid liability for selling defective cars. An experienced Palm Springs lemon law attorney knows these tactics and can fight for fair compensation.

## What is the time limit to file a Palm Springs lemon law claim?

In California, you have four years to file a lemon law claim. We recommend filing the claim as quickly as possible to avoid complications. The longer you wait, the more difficult the process becomes. More importantly, driving a defective vehicle puts both you and others at risk.

## What if I want a lemon law replacement instead of a buyback?

Buying a defective vehicle is very rare - and all manufacturers make mistakes from time to time. If you want to remain loyal to the car brand, you can request a replacement vehicle from the manufacturer, which could be the same model or one of equal value. However, manufacturers are only legally required to offer a _buyback_. Both you and the automaker must mutually agree to a replacement.

## Does Palm Springs lemon law apply to private car sales?

Generally no. Nearly all private car sales are carried out with an implied “as is” agreement. If the vehicle experiences issues immediately after purchase, you likely won’t have legal recourse under the state’s lemon law.

## How long does it take to settle a Palm Springs lemon law case?

Lemon law cases can usually be resolved within a couple of months, though some can take longer. The timeline can vary depending on the specifics of each case. Every claim is unique, and the resolution time depends on various factors, including the complexity of the defect and the manufacturer’s response.
